1
00:00:05,407 --> 00:00:10,765
America's most famous sleep story is about a man who naps for twenty years.

2
00:00:11,121 --> 00:00:16,439
This guide covers Irving's tale and a version where Rip sleeps for only one night.

3
00:00:17,456 --> 00:00:21,315
Hello, and welcome to Unwritten Chapters, Study Edition.

4
00:00:21,546 --> 00:00:26,216
In the next few minutes, I'll walk you through Rip Van Winkle, by Washington Irving.

5
00:00:26,393 --> 00:00:31,056
It's a quick guide you can use for class, a book club, or your own curiosity.

6
00:00:31,226 --> 00:00:35,311
The full guide, with ten museum and archive sources, is linked below.

7
00:00:37,356 --> 00:00:43,520
Rip Van Winkle is a kind, lazy man in a Dutch village at the foot of the Catskill Mountains.

8
00:00:43,936 --> 00:00:49,991
He'll help anyone with their work but never does his own, and his wife scolds him constantly.

9
00:00:50,343 --> 00:00:54,783
One autumn day he walks up into the mountains with his dog, Wolf.

10
00:00:55,138 --> 00:01:03,081
A strange little man asks for help carrying a keg, and leads him to a hollow where men in old Dutch clothes are silently playing

11
00:01:03,081 --> 00:01:03,889
ninepins.

12
00:01:04,276 --> 00:01:07,098
Rip drinks from their flagon and falls asleep.

13
00:01:07,842 --> 00:01:09,843
He wakes twenty years later.

14
00:01:10,013 --> 00:01:14,927
His gun has rusted, his dog is gone, and his beard is a foot long.

15
00:01:15,243 --> 00:01:20,259
In the village, King George's picture on the inn sign has become General Washington.

16
00:01:20,593 --> 00:01:23,884
The American Revolution has come and gone while he slept.

17
00:01:24,506 --> 00:01:28,169
Next, who's who, so you can keep everyone straight.

18
00:01:30,409 --> 00:01:31,639
Rip Van Winkle.

19
00:01:31,785 --> 00:01:35,989
A good-natured villager who avoids work and loves telling stories.

20
00:01:36,733 --> 00:01:38,214
Dame Van Winkle.

21
00:01:38,484 --> 00:01:41,998
Rip's wife, who has to run the farm he neglects.

22
00:01:42,793 --> 00:01:45,348
Wolf. Rip's loyal dog.

23
00:01:46,117 --> 00:01:47,341
The little men.

24
00:01:47,728 --> 00:01:52,602
Ghostly sailors said to be Henry Hudson's crew from the ship the Half Moon.

25
00:01:54,781 --> 00:01:56,295
Now for the story.

26
00:01:56,475 --> 00:01:59,479
Here's how it unfolds, chapter by chapter.

27
00:01:59,978 --> 00:02:01,077
Chapter one.

28
00:02:01,233 --> 00:02:02,972
The Kaatskill Mountains.

29
00:02:03,815 --> 00:02:06,304
Irving begins his story like this.

30
00:02:06,992 --> 00:02:10,403
Kaatskill is the old Dutch spelling of Catskill.

31
00:02:10,710 --> 00:02:14,819
These mountains rise on the west side of the Hudson River in New York.

32
00:02:15,226 --> 00:02:18,840
Irving says they change colour with the weather and the time of day.

33
00:02:19,217 --> 00:02:24,451
The good wives of the villages used them like a barometer, which is a tool for telling the weather.

34
00:02:24,802 --> 00:02:27,876
When the mountains were clear and blue, it would be fair.

35
00:02:28,231 --> 00:02:31,540
When grey clouds gathered on top, a storm was coming.

36
00:02:32,278 --> 00:02:36,185
At the foot of those mountains was a little village of old houses.

37
00:02:36,405 --> 00:02:41,168
The first Dutch settlers had built them with small yellow bricks and latticed windows.

38
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,853
And in that village lived a man named Rip Van Winkle.

39
00:02:45,316 --> 00:02:46,492
Chapter two.

40
00:02:46,769 --> 00:02:48,388
Thunder in the Hills.

41
00:02:49,032 --> 00:02:54,060
Rip looked around, but he saw nothing except a crow flying across the mountain.

42
00:02:54,386 --> 00:02:56,256
He thought he must have imagined it.

43
00:02:56,617 --> 00:03:01,525
Then he heard the same cry again, ringing through the still evening air.

44
00:03:01,811 --> 00:03:03,250
"Rip Van Winkle!

45
00:03:03,250 --> 00:03:04,767
Rip Van Winkle!"

46
00:03:05,336 --> 00:03:11,338
Wolf bristled up his back, gave a low growl, and crept close to his master's side.

47
00:03:12,073 --> 00:03:19,424
Then Rip saw a strange figure slowly climbing the rocks towards him, bent under something he carried on his back.

48
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:24,864
It was a short, square old man with thick bushy hair and a grizzled beard.

49
00:03:25,219 --> 00:03:28,432
His clothes had gone out of fashion a hundred years before.

50
00:03:28,768 --> 00:03:35,314
He wore a cloth jacket and wide breeches with buttons down the sides, and on his shoulder he carried a keg.

51
00:03:35,990 --> 00:03:38,769
Chapter three. Morning.

52
00:03:39,711 --> 00:03:43,059
When Rip opened his eyes, it was morning.

53
00:03:43,374 --> 00:03:49,392
He was lying on the green knoll at the top of the cliff, where he'd first seen the old man with the keg.

54
00:03:49,890 --> 00:03:55,409
The sun was just coming up over the mountains, and birds were hopping and twittering in the bushes.

55
00:03:55,896 --> 00:04:00,016
An eagle wheeled high overhead, catching the first of the light.

56
00:04:00,423 --> 00:04:04,382
Wolf sat beside him, wagging his tail and licking his face.

57
00:04:05,083 --> 00:04:08,042
Rip sat up slowly and rubbed his eyes.

58
00:04:08,809 --> 00:04:14,004
His gun lay beside him on the grass, as clean and bright as when he'd set it down.

59
00:04:14,361 --> 00:04:18,441
His beard was the same short, scrubby beard it had always been.

60
00:04:18,750 --> 00:04:21,273
The glen below was quiet and empty.

61
00:04:21,551 --> 00:04:26,309
There were no little men and no ninepins, no keg and no thunder.

62
00:04:26,606 --> 00:04:32,322
There was only a stream running down among the rocks, and the mist lifting slowly off the valley.

63
00:04:32,739 --> 00:04:34,276
The Last Page.

64
00:04:34,821 --> 00:04:38,719
Dame Van Winkle was standing in the doorway when he came up the lane.

65
00:04:38,719 --> 00:04:42,438
She had her hands on her hips and her mouth already open.

66
00:04:42,627 --> 00:04:47,633
She was ready to tell him what she thought about husbands who stayed out all night in the mountains.

67
00:04:48,010 --> 00:04:50,857
And Rip did something he'd never done before.

68
00:04:51,149 --> 00:04:55,702
He walked straight up to her, kissed her on the cheek, and said he was sorry.

69
00:04:56,020 --> 00:05:03,162
Before she could get over her surprise, he went round the back of the house, picked up the hammer, and began to mend the fence.

70
00:05:03,783 --> 00:05:07,892
Next, the big ideas, the ones that come up in class.

71
00:05:10,038 --> 00:05:11,689
Time and change.

72
00:05:11,924 --> 00:05:17,212
The village changes from a British colony to part of the United States while Rip sleeps.

73
00:05:17,373 --> 00:05:20,552
Irving shows history happening to ordinary people.

74
00:05:21,041 --> 00:05:21,956
Identity.

75
00:05:22,317 --> 00:05:28,932
When Rip says "I'm not myself," he means the world has changed so much he doesn't know where he fits.

76
00:05:29,705 --> 00:05:31,029
Responsibility.

77
00:05:31,334 --> 00:05:36,709
Our version asks what Rip would do if he got a warning instead of a lost twenty years.

78
00:05:37,127 --> 00:05:38,672
How stories grow.

79
00:05:39,063 --> 00:05:43,212
Irving pretends the tale was found in an old historian's papers.

80
00:05:43,511 --> 00:05:47,709
Our version shows a true story getting bigger each time it's told.

81
00:05:48,329 --> 00:05:52,158
Next, the exact moment our version changes the book.

82
00:05:54,558 --> 00:05:56,047
So what's the what-if?

83
00:05:56,086 --> 00:05:58,222
Here's the turning point we changed.

84
00:05:58,822 --> 00:06:04,132
In our story, Rip takes only one small sip and sleeps for a single night.

85
00:06:04,471 --> 00:06:09,202
But he dreams the whole twenty years, and wakes up knowing what he'd lose.

86
00:06:09,587 --> 00:06:15,632
Everything after that moment is new, but the characters still act the way their authors wrote them.

87
00:06:17,697 --> 00:06:20,693
Before we go, here's a little about who wrote it.

88
00:06:21,576 --> 00:06:27,905
Washington Irving was born in New York City in 1783, the year the Revolution ended.

89
00:06:28,126 --> 00:06:30,401
He was named after George Washington.

90
00:06:30,541 --> 00:06:37,367
He became one of the first American writers to be famous in Europe, and he spent years living in England and Spain.

91
00:06:37,652 --> 00:06:41,686
Later he settled at Sunnyside, his cottage on the Hudson River.

92
00:06:43,687 --> 00:06:48,412
Irving wrote the story while living in England, after his family's business failed.

93
00:06:48,690 --> 00:06:53,849
He set it in the Hudson Valley, where Dutch families had farmed since the 1600s.

94
00:06:54,047 --> 00:07:00,603
The story spans the years before and after the American Revolution, when the colonies became a new country.

95
00:07:02,743 --> 00:07:04,135
Want to see the real thing?

96
